🔵 **F-1646-3 (s1646 2026-08-11, MEASURED while authoring `f1643-2` — a GATE STATED IN THE WRONG UNITS: F-1643-2's quiet-machine gate is written in terms of LANES, and lanes are not the only load on this machine).** The gate reads *"author this only when all four lanes are idle and no drain is in flight"*, and its purpose is a machine quiet enough that a full-suite snapshot measures the **code** rather than the **box** (F-1270-1's load ceiling). At s1646 the letter was satisfied — all four lanes `ahead=0` USABLE, the fire's drain committed — **and the box was not quiet:** `ps` showed **Blender at 100% CPU for 2h26m** (`Blender -b assets/blender/fries-mcdx/landmark.blend`, a headless art-pipeline render) **plus `fseventsd` at 100%**, i.e. **2 of 16 physical cores saturated by work no lane instrument can see.** ⚠️ **A fire checking only `lane-usable --all` would have called the machine quiet and been wrong** — and would then have banked a load-polluted snapshot as a fresh set of *excused* reds, the outcome the RE-PRICED row calls *"strictly worse than the 13-day-old one it replaced."* ✅ **The gate's own third clause already handles this** (*"state the concurrent load it ran under"*), which is why the cure is to OBEY it rather than to rewrite it: the dispatched master records load at run start **and** end, and its scope 5 re-runs every failing spec file **serially, once**, so anything that fails parallel-but-passes-serial is declared **load-attributable** instead of entering the inventory. 🪞 **And the same trap caught its own author, which is the part worth reading:** s1646 dispatched that full-suite measurement and then started `test:ledger-guards` inside the same window — **10 chromium workers at 70–88% CPU starved the battery past 23 minutes** until it had to be abandoned and re-run guard-by-guard. **Lesson, general: "are the lanes idle?" is a cheap proxy for "is the machine quiet?", and the two answers diverge exactly when it matters most.** Ask `ps` for processes >